Advanced Geometric, Self-Supervised, and Multi-Modal Deep Learning Architectures for Low-Density Cognitive EEG Decoding
Introduction to the Small-Data Cognitive Decoding Paradigm
The extraction and classification of complex cognitive states from consumer-grade, low-density electroencephalography (EEG) represents a highly constrained optimization problem at the intersection of computational neuroscience and advanced machine learning. The experimental paradigm under investigation focuses on decoding a five-class cognitive framework—Mental Arithmetic, Pattern Recognition, Working Memory Retrieval, Reading Comprehension, and Sustained Attention—using a 14-channel Emotiv EPOC+ headset (10-20 International System: AF3, F7, F3, FC5, T7, P7, O1, O2, P8, T8, FC6, F4, F8, AF4) sampled at 128 Hz. While classical machine learning baselines relying on feature engineering (e.g., Tuned LightGBM utilizing Differential Entropy and Power Spectral Density) have established a functional baseline test accuracy of 73.34%, advancing beyond this threshold necessitates the deployment of deep learning architectures capable of modeling complex spatiotemporal neurodynamics.

However, architecting deep neural networks for this specific paradigm is severely hindered by two primary bottlenecks. The first is the small-data and low-density constraint. The dataset consists of a small-to-medium cohort of approximately 25 to 30 participants, yielding 2,908 synchronized 4-second trials that have been augmented into 37,804 sliding sub-windows (1-second duration, 75% overlap). Training high-capacity models on such limited data invariably leads to catastrophic overfitting. The second bottleneck is the neural fingerprint and inter-subject variability phenomenon. EEG signals are highly sensitive to individualized anatomical variances, such as skull impedance, cortical folding, and baseline spectral power differences. Deep learning models frequently exploit these biometric signatures, achieving artificially high accuracy by memorizing subject identities rather than learning the invariant neural correlates of the target cognitive states. When applied to an unseen user, performance degrades catastrophically due to severe domain shifts.   

This report provides an exhaustive, mathematically grounded investigation into cutting-edge deep learning methodologies spanning the 2024–2026 literature. By synthesizing advancements in Spatial-Temporal Graph Neural Networks, Large Brain Foundation Models, Riemannian Geometry, Disentangled Representation Learning, and Multi-Modal Cross-Attention, this analysis formulates strategies to explicitly overcome the dual bottlenecks of data scarcity and inter-subject variability, culminating in the proposition of three novel architectures tailored for top-tier academic dissemination.

Pillar A: Dynamic Functional Connectivity & Spatial-Temporal Graph Neural Networks
Traditional Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) operate on Euclidean grids, making them fundamentally misaligned with the non-Euclidean, functionally distributed network architecture of the human brain. Cognitive processes are not highly localized; for example, Mental Arithmetic engages the working memory networks within the Dorsolateral Prefrontal Cortex (DLPFC), while Pattern Recognition relies heavily on occipital-parietal visual-spatial reasoning networks. Spatial-Temporal Graph Neural Networks (ST-GNNs) provide an optimal mathematical framework for modeling these long-range cortical dependencies.

