Pillar B: Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) & EEG Foundation Model Adaptation
The generation of robust representations from a constrained dataset of 37,804 samples requires leveraging external, generalized priors. EEG Foundation Models, trained via Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) on thousands of hours of heterogeneous data, provide an unprecedented regularization mechanism against overfitting.   

Adapting Large Brain Models to 14-Channel Montages
Recent architectures, such as the Large Brain Model (LaBraM), BIOT, and BENDR, utilize transformer-based backbones to learn universal EEG representations. LaBraM, for instance, segments raw continuous EEG signals X∈R 
C×T
  into non-overlapping temporal windows (e.g., 200 ms patches, equating to 25 time steps at 128 Hz). These patches are processed by 1D convolutional layers to produce dimensional embeddings.   

A significant architectural hurdle is channel adaptation. Foundation models are typically pretrained on standardized 64-channel or 19-channel clinical montages. Rigid-montage models, which expect a fixed input tensor size, fail when confronted with a 14-channel Emotiv dataset. Modern frameworks solve this spatial mismatch through two primary methods:   

Spherical Spline Interpolation (SSI) and Channel Alignment: Methods like LEAD and MIRepNet project the 14 physical channels into a 64-channel virtual montage. By calculating the spatial distances between the 14 Emotiv coordinates and standard 10-20 coordinates on a 3D head model, interpolation algorithms reconstruct the missing channels, providing the foundation model with its expected input shape while minimizing spatial aliasing.   

