SSL Pretext Tasks for From-Scratch Training
If computational constraints or domain-shift issues prevent the deployment of massive foundation models, training a custom SSL model from scratch on the 37,804 samples is viable. The most effective pretext paradigms combine Masked Autoencoding (MAE) with Contrastive Learning.   

Masked Autoencoding (EEG-MAE):
Inspired by BENDR and EEG2Rep, the raw EEG is transformed into a continuous latent space. A large fraction (e.g., 60-75%) of the temporal patches is randomly masked. Crucially, instead of reconstructing the noisy raw voltage waveforms, the transformer decoder is tasked with predicting the original discrete neural codes (generated via Vector-Quantized Neural Spectrum Prediction, VQ-NSP) or latent embeddings of the masked patches. This forces the network to learn the underlying semantic structure of the cognitive state rather than memorizing high-frequency noise.   

