Superior Robustness against Inter-Subject Noise
By forcing the neural network to calculate decision boundaries based on Riemannian geodesic distances—specifically utilizing the Affine Invariant Riemannian Metric (AIRM) or the Log-Cholesky Metric (LCM)—the network becomes structurally invariant to linear spatial transformations. In the context of EEG, variations in sensor placement, skull thickness, and global impedance manifest as linear transformations of the covariance matrix. Because Riemannian metrics compute the distance between two covariance matrices independently of these affine transformations, SPD networks inherently strip away a massive portion of the subject-specific neural fingerprint. This theoretical property translates into empirical superiority in cross-subject generalization, making Riemannian geometry an indispensable tool for low-density BCI development.   

