Pillar E: Multi-Modal Cross-Attention (Neural EEG + Behavioral Context)
In advanced cognitive neuroscience, behavioral metadata—such as stimulus difficulty (categorical: easy/medium/hard), reaction time (continuous scalar in milliseconds), and task correctness (binary 0/1)—are deeply entangled with neural activity. Relying on EEG alone discards valuable contextual priors. However, carelessly concatenating this tabular data with dense neural embeddings risks "shortcut learning," a failure mode where the model ignores the noisy EEG entirely and predicts the cognitive state based solely on behavioral metadata (e.g., learning that long reaction times equal high difficulty tasks).

