Synthesis
Transcending the 73.34% classical machine learning baseline on a low-density, 14-channel Emotiv dataset requires abandoning standard Euclidean deep learning paradigms, which inevitably succumb to catastrophic overfitting and the neural fingerprint bottleneck.

The comprehensive mathematical investigation detailed in this report demonstrates that extracting pure, task-invariant cognitive states requires manipulating the underlying geometric space of the data. Deploying Spatial-Temporal Graph Neural Networks equipped with dynamic, self-attention-derived adjacency matrices effectively compensates for low spatial resolution by uncovering hidden functional connectivity graphs. Concurrently, mapping Spatial Covariance Matrices to Symmetric Positive Definite (SPD) manifolds and optimizing decision boundaries via Riemannian metrics intrinsically strips away linear subject-specific spatial variations, neutralizing the inter-subject variability bottleneck.

Furthermore, the data scarcity constraint is best overcome by leveraging Large Brain Models adapted via spatial embeddings and LoRA, or through Hybrid Masked Autoencoding and Contrastive Learning. Coupling these robust neural encoders with a Domain-Adversarial Gradient Reversal Layer ensures cross-subject domain generalization, while Dynamic Gated Cross-Attention allows the safe, non-corrupting integration of behavioral metadata. Implementing any of the three proposed architectures (R-STGNN, D-LaBraM-Gate, or CL-SPD-Proto) provides a rigorous, mathematically sound pathway to state-of-the-art cognitive state decoding, elevating the research trajectory to the highest echelons of academic publishing.


